---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Heading from "../components/Heading.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import { PACKAGES, NPM, REPO, GODOC, GITHUB_ORG, SITE_TAGLINE, SPONSOR, AUTHOR } from "../consts";
import emblem from "../assets/brand/mark.svg";

const core = PACKAGES.filter(
  (p) => p.tier === "engine" || p.tier === "tooling" || p.tier === "agent",
);
const grammars = PACKAGES.filter((p) => p.tier === "grammar");
---

<Base wide>
  <!-- What it is, in one sentence. -->
  <section class="intro">
    <img class="emblem" src={emblem.src} width="128" height="128" alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
    <Heading level={1} id="tabnas">{SITE_TAGLINE}</Heading>
    <p class="lede">
      <i>tabnas</i> is a parser VM: a simple state and lookup engine that
      grammars compile down to. Because a grammar is just data, you extend one
      that already works rather than starting over — and because the data is
      flat and regular, an agent can write one. <i>tabnas</i> has already
      burned the tokens, so you don't have to.
    </p>
    <!-- Abridged copy of example 3 below: the shortest thing that shows the
         actual argument — RFC 5234 ABNF in, behaviour bound by reference, a
         value out. Sources live in examples/abnf-actions/ and are executed by
         tools/test-examples.mjs, so the TS and Go versions are known to agree
         rather than claimed to. -->
    <div class="hero-code">
      <CodeTabs id="abnf-actions" label="ABNF in, a parser out" lines={[[1, 18]]} />
      <p class="code-note">
        Valid RFC 5234 — nothing added to it. Behaviour binds out of band,
        through names the compiler already assigns.
        <a href="#a-grammar-end-to-end">See it end to end ↓</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="meta">
      MIT licensed ·
      <a href={GITHUB_ORG} target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/tabnas</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- The motivation, condensed from /why. -->
  <section class="why">
    <Heading level={2} id="why-tabnas">Why <i>tabnas</i></Heading>
    <div class="why-body">
      <p>
        Writing a parser is expensive, so most languages get one and that's
        that. Parser toolkits hand you generated code from a grammar file, or
        you write recursive descent by hand — and either way, extending
        someone else's language means forking their parser and owning the
        fork.
      </p>
      <p>
        <i>tabnas</i> started as an attempt to write a more forgiving JSON parser and
        turned into an extensible one by accident. The engine is a for-loop
        over tokens with a rule stack, and the grammar is the lookup table it
        walks. So adding a language feature means adding rules and token
        alternates to a table. That is the whole extension mechanism: JSON is a
        handful of rules, JSONC adds comments to them, jsonic relaxes the
        quoting, YAML keeps going. Every grammar is a plugin over another
        plugin.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second consequence took longer to notice. A machine this plain is a
        good <em>compile target</em>. ABNF compiles into it, so a human can
        write a grammar in the notation the RFCs already use. And the grammar
        itself is flat, regular, declarative data — which is exactly the kind
        of thing a language model is good at emitting, and which you can check
        before you run it. Defining a grammar this way is surprisingly not too
        hard for humans, and really easy for agents.
      </p>
      <p>
        Put those together and the cost of parsing something new drops a long
        way. That is the whole point of the project.
      </p>
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/why">Read the whole story — how the algorithm works, and where it came from</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="tldr">
      <p class="tldr-head">The technical TLDR</p>
      <p>
        You write a for-loop over parsing tokens. There's no recursion — a
        stack keeps track of rule depth. Each rule represents a semantic
        element of your language, and you move to the next rule using a lookup
        table of token alternates. Rules are hit twice, once on descent and
        once on ascent, so they have two states, open and close, and two token
        lookup tables.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <!-- What the engine actually gives you. -->
  <section class="features">
    <Heading level={2} id="core-features">Core features</Heading>
    <div class="grid feat">
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="extend-dont-fork">Extend, don't fork</Heading>
        <p>
          Every grammar is a plugin over another. Add rules and token
          alternates to a language that already parses, instead of taking
          ownership of someone else's parser.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="a-target-agents-can-hit">A target agents can hit</Heading>
        <p>
          A grammar is flat declarative data — rules, token alternates,
          actions. That's a shape a model can emit reliably and you can check
          before running. The whole workflow installs into an agent, as
          <a href="/skills">skills</a> and an <a href="/mcp">MCP server</a> —
          <a href="/agents">agents</a> has the commands.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="a-target-compilers-can-hit">A target compilers can hit</Heading>
        <p>
          <a href={REPO("abnf")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">@tabnas/abnf</a>
          compiles RFC 5234 ABNF into a working grammar, left recursion
          included — so an RFC's own notation is runnable.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="three-levels">Three levels</Heading>
        <p>
          ABNF when a human is writing, declarative JSON when an agent is, and
          a programmatic API for parameterised grammars — the level
          <a href={REPO("expr")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">expr</a> and
          <a href={REPO("directive")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">directive</a>
          are built at.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="nothing-is-generated">Nothing is generated</Heading>
        <p>
          The grammar is walked at runtime, so it can be inspected while it
          runs: <a href={REPO("debug")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">debug</a>
          describes a live grammar and prints it back as ABNF,
          <a href={REPO("railroad")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">railroad</a>
          draws it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="runs-where-you-do">Runs where you do</Heading>
        <p>
          The engine is implemented in TypeScript and in Go, both driven by the
          same grammars and checked against the same fixtures.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Structure twice, then behaviour — the compile-target argument, shown. -->
  <section class="example">
    <Heading level={2} id="a-grammar-end-to-end">A grammar, end to end</Heading>
    <p class="sec-note">
      Addition, four times: the grammar as a person writes it, the same
      grammar as data, and the same two actions attached two ways — by
      reference to the ABNF, and inline on the rule table. Steps 3 and 4
      compute the same total with the same logic, because the ABNF compiles
      to the table.
    </p>

    <div class="code-cols">
      <div>
        <p class="code-label">1 · ABNF — as a person writes it</p>
        <CodeTabs id="abnf-text" />
        <p class="code-note">
          <code>NR</code> is the built-in number token, <code>[ … ]</code> is
          optional, and <code>add</code> refers to itself to take a whole
          chain. <code>val</code> wraps it — that's where the total will live.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="code-label">2 · The same grammar, as data</p>
        <CodeTabs id="grammar-data" />
        <p class="code-note">
          <code>p</code> pushes a rule, <code>s</code> matches tokens,
          <code>r</code> repeats the rule at the same depth, and
          <code>{"{}"}</code> is the alternate that ends it. This recognises
          <code>1+2+3</code> and rejects <code>1+*</code> — but produces
          nothing. It's the shape an agent emits.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="actions-block">
      <p class="code-label">3 · Actions by reference — the <code>@ref</code> form</p>
      <div class="code-cols narrow">
        <div>
          {/* Same example as the hero, shown in full here — `instance` keeps
              the two tab bars from sharing a radio group. */}
          <CodeTabs id="abnf-actions" instance="full" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="code-note wide">
            The grammar is exactly the ABNF from step 1 — valid RFC 5234,
            nothing added to it. Behaviour binds out of band, through names the
            compiler already assigns: <code>'@val:o:add'</code> is the
            <code>val</code> rule's alternate that pushes <code>add</code>;
            <code>'@add:o:NR'</code> is the <code>add</code> rule's alternate
            on an <code>NR</code> token. Ask the compiler which marks a grammar
            has with <code>tabnas-abnf --marks</code> rather than guessing.
          </p>
          <p class="code-note wide">
            These are <strong>the same two actions, on the same two rules, as
            step 4 below</strong> — because the compiler emits the same shape.
            The tail self-reference <code>[ PL add ]</code> compiles to the
            <code>r: 'add'</code> close-phase repeat that step 4 declares by
            hand, so <code>r.parent</code> is <code>val</code> for every
            repetition, and the total lands on <code>val</code>'s node, where
            <code>parse</code> returns it.
          </p>
          <p class="code-note wide">
            This is what keeps the grammar a compile target: the text a person
            or an agent writes never has to carry code, so it stays printable,
            drawable, and checkable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="actions-block">
      <p class="code-label">4 · Actions inline — carried on the parse</p>
      <div class="code-cols narrow">
        <div>
          <CodeTabs id="inline-actions" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="code-note wide">
            Same grammar, same machine — the actions are just written straight
            onto the alternates as <code>a</code>. The total now rides on the
            parse instead of an outer variable, so <code>parse</code> returns
            it directly and concurrent parses can't collide.
          </p>
          <p class="code-note wide">
            Because <code>r</code> repeats <code>add</code> at the same stack
            depth rather than nesting it, every <code>add</code> shares one
            parent — so the accumulator is a single number and each action is a
            single expression. <code>r.node</code> is the value a rule carries;
            <code>r.o</code> the tokens matched in the open phase, so
            <code>r.o[0].val</code> is the number just read.
          </p>
          <p class="code-note wide">
            The <a href="/docs/quickstart">quickstart</a> walks through the
            <code>@ref</code> form, and the <a href="/playground">playground</a>
            runs a grammar in the browser.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="install">
      <span class="code-label">Install</span>
      <code>npm install @tabnas/parser @tabnas/abnf</code>
      <span class="or">or</span>
      <code>go get github.com/tabnas/parser/go</code>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- The packages. -->
  <section class="packages">
    <Heading level={2} id="packages">Packages</Heading>
    <p class="sec-note">
      A small core engine, tools for writing grammars and for connecting
      agents, and {grammars.length} languages built on top — most of them
      extensions of another one in the list. The
      <a href="/releases">releases</a> page has all {PACKAGES.length}, their
      versions, and which registries each one is on.
    </p>

    <div class="grid pkgs">
      {core.map((p) => (
        <article class="pkg">
          <Heading level={3} id={p.name}>@tabnas/{p.name}</Heading>
          <p>{p.blurb}</p>
          <p class="pkg-links">
            <a href={REPO(p.name)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>
            {p.npm && <a href={NPM(p.name)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm</a>}
            {p.go && <a href={GODOC(p.name)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">go</a>}
          </p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="chips">
      <span class="chips-label">Languages:</span>
      {grammars.map((p) => (
        <a class="chip" href={REPO(p.name)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{p.name}</a>
      ))}
      <a class="chip more" href="/releases">all {PACKAGES.length} packages</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Who works on it, and how to join in. -->
  <section class="project">
    <Heading level={2} id="the-project">The project</Heading>
    <div class="grid proj">
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="who-writes-it">Who writes it</Heading>
        <p>
          <i>tabnas</i> is written by
          <a href={AUTHOR.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{AUTHOR.name}</a>
          (<a href={AUTHOR.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener">@rjrodger</a>),
          who also wrote <a href="https://senecajs.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Seneca</a>
          and jsonic — which is where this came from.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="contributing">Contributing</Heading>
        <p>
          This project is AI-friendly: contributions written with an agent are
          welcome, and every package repository ships an <code>AGENTS.md</code>
          to point one in the right direction. Work lands in TypeScript first,
          then Go to match. See <a href="/community">community</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <Heading level={3} id="licence-and-funding">Licence and funding</Heading>
        <p>
          MIT, across every repository in the
          <a href={GITHUB_ORG} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i>tabnas</i> org</a>.
          Development is sponsored by
          <a href={SPONSOR.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{SPONSOR.name}</a> —
          see <a href="/sponsors">sponsors</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section { padding: 0 0 3.5rem; }

  /* Intro */
  .intro { text-align: center; padding: 2.5rem 0 3.5rem; }
  .intro .emblem {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 6px 20px rgba(11, 45, 26, 0.28));
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .intro :global(h1) {
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3.2rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    text-wrap: balance;
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin: 0 auto 1rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  /* Abridged example 3, above the fold. Left-aligned inside the centred
     intro — code centred with the prose reads as decoration, not as code. */
  .hero-code {
    text-align: left;
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: 2.25rem auto 0;
  }
  .hero-code pre {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .hero-code pre > code { color: var(--ink); }
  .hero-code .code-note { max-width: none; }

  .meta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-mute);
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  section :global(h2) {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  section :global(h3) { font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 0.35rem; }
  .sec-note { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.98rem; max-width: 68ch; }

  /* Why */
  .why { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.35fr 0.65fr; gap: 0.75rem 2.5rem; align-items: start; }
  .why :global(h2) { grid-column: 1 / -1; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .why-body p { color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 62ch; }
  .why-body .more { font-family: var(--font-heading); font-style: italic; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .tldr {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-strong);
    padding: 0 0 0 1.25rem;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
  .tldr p { color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; }
  .tldr-head {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--accent) !important;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem !important;
  }

  /* Grids */
  .grid { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem 2.25rem; }
  .grid.feat { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .grid.proj { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .grid p { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.98rem; margin: 0; }

  /* Example */
  /* minmax(0, …) not 1fr: a bare 1fr takes its minimum from the content, so
     the widest line of the rule-table block sized the column and the page
     scrolled sideways below ~653px. The `pre` is overflow-x: auto, so with a
     zero minimum the code scrolls inside its own box instead. */
  .code-cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1.5rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; align-items: start; }
  .code-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .example pre {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .example pre > code { color: var(--ink); }
  .code-note { color: var(--ink-mute); font-size: 0.85rem; margin: 0; max-width: 44ch; }
  .install {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .install code { font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .install .or { color: var(--ink-mute); font-size: 0.85rem; }

  /* Packages */
  .grid.pkgs { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr)); gap: 1.25rem; }
  .pkg :global(h3) { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0 0 0.3rem; }
  .pkg-links { display: flex; gap: 0.85rem; margin-top: 0.4rem !important; font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.5rem; margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .chips-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; color: var(--ink-mute);
  }
  .chip {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft); text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 999px; padding: 0.25rem 0.7rem;
  }
  .chip:hover { color: var(--primary); border-color: var(--border-strong); }
  .chip.more { border-style: dashed; color: var(--primary); }

  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .why { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.75rem; }
    .grid.feat, .grid.proj { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .code-cols { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .grid.feat, .grid.proj { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
